      "adaptations": "Foundation: short distance, walking pace, wide target. Intermediate: full pace and one decision. Advanced: time, precision, or complex sequence. Large group: six lanes. Small: pairs. Limited space: short stations. Partial equipment: share in pairs. Varied needs: visual mark, extra time, fixed partner, adapted range.",
      "assessment": "Checks: technique, control, regulation, safety, independence. Success: 4–5 criteria. Partial: 2–3 with prompt. Not yet: 0–1 or unsafe; return to foundation version.",
      "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Was load appropriate? Students: What evidence shows improvement and what is next? Next: revisit one criterion.",
      "continuity": "If met: FIT-04 — self-regulated partner circuit. If not: return to foundation version, reduce distance or load, and model again.",
      "bankLink": "Tags: fitness, aerobic intervals, endurance, effort regulation, school PE.\nSport: Fitness. Age: 11–15. Equipment: cones, mats, timer, level cards.\nSkills: pacing, effort rating, active recovery, interval structure, self-adjustment.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: aerobic intervals, work-to-recovery ratio, personal pace, 1–5 effort scale, active recovery, consistency, repeatable effort.",
      "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
      "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? Interval work is the most practical way to build cardiovascular fitness in a mixed-ability class, because every student works at their own pace inside the same structure and nobody is left behind or held back.\nWhat develops? Pace selection and repeatability, honest effort rating, active recovery between efforts, and the self-management to adjust a load mid-session rather than endure a bad choice.\nWhy not skip? Without interval structure, cardiovascular work in school becomes a single long run in which the fittest students are barely challenged and the least fit are publicly exposed; the interval format gives both a genuine session.",
