      "reflection": "Teacher: Who acted independently? Was load appropriate? Students: What evidence shows improvement and what is next? Next: revisit one criterion.",
      "continuity": "If met: FIT-05 — designing a balanced personal circuit. If not: return to foundation version, reduce distance or load, and model again.",
      "bankLink": "Tags: fitness, partner circuit, strength, endurance, peer coaching, school PE.\nSport: Fitness. Age: 12–16. Equipment: cones, mats, timer, level cards.\nSkills: load selection, movement quality, peer observation, descriptive feedback, role rotation, self-adjustment.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: partner circuit, performer and observer, level card, descriptive feedback, respecting a partner's level, one-variable change, self-regulation.",
      "systemData": "Physical: moderate. Cognitive: moderate. Organizational: moderate. Independence: medium.",
      "teacherSummary": "State goal and cues.\nWarm up gradually.\nBuild technique without pressure.\nAdd decision and challenge.\nClose with evidence and goal.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why important? It turns fitness from a private struggle into a shared, coached activity, and it teaches the social skill that makes partner work safe: respecting somebody else's chosen level instead of imposing your own.\nWhat develops? Observation of a specific technical cue, descriptive rather than evaluative feedback, independent load selection while being watched, and evidence-based adjustment across rounds.\nWhy not skip? Partner work introduced without these rules becomes a competition in which the less fit student is pushed to match the fitter one — which is where technique collapses, injuries happen, and students learn to dislike fitness training.",
