      "adaptations": "Foundation: 20–25 seconds work, smaller range, and wall support.\nIntermediate: 30–40 seconds and a controlled full version.\nAdvanced: independent planning, evidence-based explanation, or longer work without quality loss.\nLarge group: trios with performer, observer, recorder; duplicate simple stations.\nSmall group: fewer stations and more feedback rounds.\nLimited space: stationary actions and a short one-way walking route.\nPartial equipment: use a wall, court lines, and body weight; a mat is not essential at every station.\nStudents with different needs: adapt position, range, time, pace, role, and communication; allow seated performance or a consistent partner.",
      "assessment": "Teacher checks: choice, technique, regulation, safety, and evidence-based explanation.\nSuccess: plans, performs safely, adjusts one variable, and explains why.\nPartial success: performs safely but needs a prompt to choose or explain.\nNot yet successful: continues despite risk cues, loses spacing, or cannot perform the foundation version after adaptation; stop and return to individual instruction.",
      "reflection": "Teacher questions: Who selected a suitable load? Who changed in response to evidence? Were transitions and work–recovery ratios safe?\nStudent questions: What did you plan? What happened? Which adjustment will you retain?\nNext improvement: change one station, one timing ratio, or one feedback prompt before FIT-08 — cardiorespiratory endurance and the talk test.",
      "continuity": "If outcomes are met: proceed to FIT-08 — cardiorespiratory endurance and the talk test.\nIf not: return to the foundation option, a short round with one measure, and guided choice before repeating the lesson.",
      "bankLink": "Tags: fitness, school, self-regulation, safety, learning pathway.\nSport: physical fitness. Age: 12–16. Equipment: basic.\nSkills: planning, performance, observation, feedback, adjustment.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: muscular endurance, movement quality under fatigue, alignment, breathing on effort, stopping before failure, level selection, quality markers.",
      "systemData": "Physical load: moderate\nCognitive load: moderate\nOrganisational load: moderate\nStudent independence: medium–high",
      "teacherSummary": "3 minutes for goal and safety; 7 minutes for gradual warm-up.\n10 minutes to learn the quality measure and choose a level.\n12 minutes of multi-round practice with one adjustment.\n8-minute applied challenge with no performance ranking.\n5 minutes for reflection, recovery, and orderly collection.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why is it important? Muscular endurance is where technique is genuinely tested: anyone can hold good form for three repetitions, but holding it for thirty is what actually protects the body under load.\nWhat does it develop? Trunk stability and alignment sustained through fatigue, breathing that continues under effort, recognition of the moment quality begins to drop, and the judgement to stop or step down rather than push through.\nWhy not skip it? Endurance work introduced without a quality rule becomes a repetition contest in which form collapses in the final third — precisely where injury risk rises, and where students learn a habit they will repeat in a gym years later.",
