      "bankLink": "Tags: fitness, school, self-regulation, safety, learning pathway.\nSport: physical fitness. Age: 12–16. Equipment: basic.\nSkills: planning, performance, observation, feedback, adjustment.\nLoad: moderate. Complexity: intermediate.\nKeywords: summative assessment, independent planning, balanced session, self-regulation, evidence-based reflection, process criteria, personal progress.",
      "systemData": "Physical load: moderate\nCognitive load: moderate\nOrganisational load: moderate\nStudent independence: medium–high",
      "teacherSummary": "3 minutes for goal and safety; 7 minutes for gradual warm-up.\n10 minutes to learn the quality measure and choose a level.\n12 minutes of multi-round practice with one adjustment.\n8-minute applied challenge with no performance ranking.\n5 minutes for reflection, recovery, and orderly collection.",
      "pedagogicalValue": "Why is it important? It is the only point at which a teacher can see whether a student can run a safe, balanced session without being told how — which is the actual aim of a school fitness unit, rather than a fitness score.\nWhat does it develop? Independent planning of a balanced session, self-regulation without supervision, honest recording, and the ability to explain a decision from evidence rather than from how it felt.\nWhy not skip it? A fitness unit assessed on performance measures rewards students who were already fit on day one and tells nobody what was learned; assessing planning and regulation instead measures what the ten lessons actually taught.",
